// CT07's case since card CT07-TWO-THREAD-REMAP (wave 37). The scenario is
// "two disjoint array halves mutated independently", and this is the first
// artifact on which "independently" means *two threads*.
//
// Wave 28 mapped CT07 onto two disjoint READ loans; wave 30 (CT07-WRITE-REMAP)
// remapped it onto two WRITE authorities over disjoint ranges — but both were
// bindings of one sequential entry, and that card named the gap itself: "NOT
// two threads; the task-crossing half is residual WRITE-LEND-TASKS". Wave 31
// crossed the spawn boundary with the lease and wave 36
// (WRITE-LEND-TASKS-EXERCISE) let a worker body actually write through it.
//
// So: one allocation whose payload is a single int32_t; two children holding
// write leases over its two disjoint halves, [0, 2) and [2, 4); each child
// writing a byte through its own lease; the owner reading both bytes back only
// after both joins, which is when the leases return. Each worker writes its own
// RELATIVE byte 0, and the events name owner bytes 0 and 2 — so the rebasing
// law is visible in the observation rather than asserted about it.
//
// Disjointness is decided by ownership::axes_overlap, called by
// LeaseRange::overlaps in the scoped-task ledger; exclusivity is the ledger's,
// not a route rule's. The three rejection controls take the scenario's two
// words apart: overlapping_leases.qcore is the disjointness half, and
// past_range_byte.qcore / past_extent_read.qcore are the bounds half in each of
// the two layers that owns it (the lease's rebased containment, and the
// payload's own extent).
//
// This program is the plain scoped-task form of the flagship pinned in
// compiler/tests/write_lend_tasks_exercise.rs as DISJOINT_EXERCISE: it drops
// that program's `import std.logger;` and `log_level trace;` so the checked
// artifact is a ScopedTasks module the heap-scenario runner consumes, and it
// keeps both of the owner's byte reads. It is not byte-identical to any other
// fixture on disk — see the report's fixture-duplication disclosure.
import std.concurrency;
concurrency checked;
fn writer(half: lend<Buffer>) -> i32 {
  let done = qcore_buffer_write(half, 0, 9);
  return 1;
}
fn main() -> i32 effects { alloc, throws<AllocError>, spawn, join, may_block } {
  let buffer = qcore_buffer_new(0)?;
  thread scope s {
    match spawn s writer(lend buffer 0..2) {
      Result::Ok { value: first } => {
        match spawn s writer(lend buffer 2..4) {
          Result::Ok { value: second } => {
            let a = join move first?;
            let b = join move second?;
            let byte0 = qcore_buffer_read(buffer, 0);
            let byte2 = qcore_buffer_read(buffer, 2);
            drop buffer;
            return byte2;
          }
          Result::Err { error: refused } => {
            let a = join move first?;
            drop buffer;
            return a;
          }
        }
      }
      Result::Err { error: failure } => {
        drop buffer;
        return 0;
      }
    }
  }
}
